¶
'''bedspread''' (bed′spred), ''n.'' The uppermost quilt or covering of a bed, generally ornamental.

'''bed-spring''' (bed′spring), ''n.'' A spring, usually of spiral form, used in making spring-beds.

'''bedstaff†''' (bed′stȧf), ''n.'' A staff or stick formerly used in some way about a bed, and frequently
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Bedstaff.—From a French manuscript of the 15th century.}}}}
serving as a weapon, in which sense the word most commonly occurs. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') A bed-slat. (''b'') The stick or staff used to spread out the bedclothes in making a bed placed in a recess. (''c'') A bar or post placed at each side of a bed to keep the bedclothes from falling off. (''d'') One of the rods used in forming the “tent” in old-fashioned tent-beds.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Now do I feel the calf of my right leg
Tingle, and dwindle to th’ smallness of a ''bed-staff''.</poem>
{{right|''T. Tomkis'' (?), Albumazar, ii. 3.}}
<poem>He gives out
He’ll take a ''Bedstaff'', or an holy Wand
And baste you lustily two or three hours
Before you go to Bed, to make you limber.</poem>
{{right|''Cartwright'', Love’s Convert, iv. 1.}}}}

